### Harsh Critic
# Review of FSL-MIC: An Attentional Few-Shot Learning Framework for EEG Motor Imagery Classification

## Critical Issues

### 1. The headline claim of "significantly outperforms traditional methods" is not supported by the evidence (Structural)

The abstract states "The proposed FSL framework significantly outperforms traditional methods," but Table 1 contradicts this. The proposed RelationNet-attention at 20 shots achieves only 72.6% (BCI 2a), 73.2% (BCI 2b), and 68.2% (Experimental) — dramatically below the CNN-attention-All baseline (89.1%, 86.3%, 81.2%). More critically, the RelationNet-attention at 20 shots *does not even clearly beat* the CNN-attention-Few baseline, which uses an equivalent amount of labeled data: on the Experimental dataset, CNN-attention-Few (69.2%) actually *outperforms* RelationNet-attention 20-shot (68.2%). On BCI 2b the margin is only ~2 percentage points (73.2% vs 71.3%). A method that loses to or roughly ties a straightforward baseline trained on the same number of samples, while claiming to "significantly outperform traditional methods," misrepresents its own results.

### 2. No comparison with any established FSL or MI-EEG baselines (Structural)

The paper cites An et al. (2020, 2023) as direct prior work applying FSL to the same BCI datasets, yet includes zero numerical comparisons with those methods. The only baselines are two variants of the authors' own CNN-attention architecture. There is no comparison with: (a) any standard FSL method (Prototypical Networks, Matching Networks, MAML, the original Relation Network); (b) any published MI-EEG result on BCI 2a/2b (e.g., FBCSP, the competition winner, or any of the dozens of subsequent methods); or (c) the cited An et al. methods. Without these comparisons, there is no way to assess whether the proposed method advances the state of the art in either the FSL or MI-EEG literature. A single self-comparison does not establish contribution.

### 3. The evaluation protocol does not demonstrate the claimed few-shot generalization advantage (Structural)

The paper's core motivation is that FSL enables classification of unseen subjects with minimal data. But the experimental setup does not cleanly test this. CNN-attention-Few is trained from scratch on only 40 samples from the test subject with standard supervised learning — it is not a meta-learning method and receives no cross-subject pretraining. The fair comparison would be between different *meta-learning* approaches using the same episode structure, or between the proposed method and the same embedding network pretrained on other subjects then fine-tuned with few samples. As it stands, the "improvement" of RelationNet over CNN-attention-Few on some configurations could simply reflect the benefit of cross-subject pretraining rather than any architectural contribution. This confound invalidates attributing the gains to the attention or relation modules.

## Section-by-Section Notes

**Abstract & Introduction:** The claim that FSL "significantly outperforms traditional methods" is the paper's headline framing but is unsupported (see Critical Issue 1). The introduction positions the work as addressing "limited data" but never acknowledges that even 20-shot performance remains far below the full-data baseline, which is the actual practical question.

**Section 3.1 (Embedding Module):** The description is vague. It states the embedding reduces input dimension "100 times" but does not specify the actual input dimensions or the precise convolutional architecture (number of layers, kernel sizes, strides, padding). The referenced Figure 1 uses symbolic notation that is not fully decoded in the text. This affects reproducibility.

**Section 3.2 (Self-Attention):** The self-attention described is a standard scaled-dot-product attention over channels, without the standard 1/√d scaling factor in the softmax argument. The formula given is S = QK^T without normalization by dimension — this is important because without scaling, the dot products can have very large magnitudes pushing softmax into saturation. The paper does not discuss or justify this omission. Also, the "interpretability" claim is deferred entirely to supplementary material (single subject) and a future paper, making it an unsupported assertion in this submission.

**Section 3.3 (Relation Module):** Focal loss is used but never justified. In a balanced 2-way classification setting, the class-balancing parameter α and focusing parameter γ serve no clear purpose. The values of α and γ are never specified. The relation module concatenates support and query features "in the channel direction," but the channel counts differ across datasets (22, 3, 64) — the paper does not explain how this architectural choice accommodates variable input dimensions, or whether separate architectures are trained per dataset.

**Section 4.1 (Datasets):** BCI 2a is a 4-class dataset (left hand, right hand, feet, tongue). The paper uses it for 2-way classification but never states which classes are used or how the 4-class problem is reduced to binary. This is a significant omission — selecting only the two most discriminable classes could inflate results relative to published benchmarks that use all four.

**Section 4.2 (Experimental Setting):** The term "DA accuracy" is used throughout but never defined. It appears to mean accuracy with data augmentation, but this is not stated. If so, the standard accuracy and DA accuracy are not two separate evaluations of the same method — they are two different training conditions, and the paper treats them as parallel metrics without explanation. Also, the 9-fold / 7-fold cross-validation is described imprecisely: "each training set included all experiments from a subject except the last one, which served as the validation set" — this implies a subject's last session is validation, but the test set is the held-out subject. So there are two nested splits, but the validation set's role and the potential for data leakage are not clearly explained.

**Section 4.3 (Results):** No statistical significance tests are reported. With 7–9 subjects per dataset, the high standard deviations (e.g., ±7.2% for BCI 2b 1-shot) mean many comparisons may not be statistically distinguishable.

**Section 4.4 (Conclusion):** Claims the model "outperforms" An et al., but no direct numerical comparison is provided anywhere in the paper. This is an unsubstantiated claim.

## Strengths

1. The problem is practically important: reducing calibration time for new BCI users through few-shot learning is a meaningful goal, and the cross-subject generalization setting is the right one to evaluate this.
2. The paper provides results on three datasets including a newly collected one with 64 channels, which adds to the evaluation breadth.
3. The idea of combining attention mechanisms with relation networks for EEG is a reasonable architectural direction, and the channel-wise attention could in principle help identify task-relevant electrodes.

## Missing Parts and Places to Improve

### Missing Experiments
1. **Comparison with established FSL methods** (Prototypical Networks, MAML, original Relation Network) on the same episode structure — without this, the architectural contributions cannot be attributed to the specific design choices rather than the meta-learning paradigm itself.
2. **Comparison with published BCI 2a/2b results** — at minimum, FBCSP and the An et al. (2020, 2023) methods should be included as baselines with their reported numbers.
3. **Ablation of attention and relation modules** — the paper never isolates whether the attention module or the relation module (vs. distance-based comparison) actually contributes to performance. A RelationNet *without* attention and a Prototypical Network with attention would establish this.

### Deeper Analysis Needed
1. **Per-subject performance breakdown** — with only 7–9 subjects, aggregating hides whether the method works for a few easy subjects and fails on the rest. This is critical for clinical applicability.
2. **Analysis of which classes from BCI 2a are used** — clarifying the binary classification setup and reporting 4-class results would enable fair comparison with the literature.

### Visualizations & Case Studies
1. **Attention visualizations across multiple subjects** — the paper claims interpretability but shows results for only one subject in supplementary material. Presenting attention maps across subjects would reveal whether the attention consistently focuses on motor cortex electrodes as expected.

### Obvious Next Steps
1. **Define "DA accuracy" explicitly and either integrate augmentation into all methods or remove it as a separate column** — currently it is unclear what comparison is being drawn.

## Overall Assessment

This paper does not establish its core claims. The headline assertion that the proposed FSL framework "significantly outperforms traditional methods" is contradicted by the authors' own Table 1, where the method fails to consistently beat even a simple few-sample supervised baseline on the same data budget. No comparison with any prior FSL method or published MI-EEG result is provided, making it impossible to assess whether the contribution advances either field. The architectural novelty over a standard Relation Network with channel attention is thin and un-ablated. These issues are structural — the evaluation framework cannot support the conclusions drawn, and the missing baselines mean the paper's position in the literature is undefined. The paper should not be accepted in its current form.
### Strength Finder
## Strengths

### Core strengths (directly support the paper's main claims)

1. **Few-shot framework matches or exceeds supervised baseline with far fewer target-subject samples**: On BCI 2a, the 1-shot RelationNet-attention achieves 63.1% accuracy, slightly surpassing CNN-attention-Few (trained with 40 labeled samples from the target subject) at 62.8%; and at 20 shots, RelationNet-attention reaches 72.6% vs. 62.8%, demonstrating the FSL paradigm's effectiveness with limited target-subject data (Table 1).

2. **Consistent and monotonic improvement with increasing shots across three diverse datasets**: Table 1 shows accuracy improves steadily from 1-shot to 20-shot on every dataset (BCI 2a: 63.1%→72.6%; BCI 2b: 59.9%→73.2%; Experimental: 54.9%→68.2%), validating that the relation module meaningfully leverages additional support data rather than producing noisy or unstable gains.

3. **Generalization across datasets with fundamentally different characteristics**: The framework is evaluated on datasets spanning different channel counts and experimental conditions—22-channel no-feedback (BCI 2a), 3-channel with feedback (BCI 2b), and 64-channel (Experimental)—and maintains coherent performance trends across all three (Table 1, Figure 3), demonstrating robustness to varying EEG setups.

4. **Learned relation module instead of fixed distance metrics**: Unlike prior FSL approaches for EEG MI that rely on prototype vectors with distance-based comparisons (e.g., Burrello et al. 2019, cited in Section 2), the proposed relation module uses CNN+FC layers to learn non-linear comparators end-to-end (Section 3.3, Figure 1), which better captures complex relationships between support and query embeddings.

### Supporting strengths

1. **Self-attention provides interpretable channel-level importance**: Section 3.2 describes how attention scores over electrode channels can be visualized to show which electrodes drive predictions, and Supplementary Material includes a heatmap visualization for a representative subject, offering neuroscientific interpretability beyond standard post-hoc methods like Grad-CAM.

2. **Differentiation from closest prior work (An et al. 2023)**: Section 4.4 identifies two concrete differences—dataset-specific data augmentation (rather than fine-tuning) and a tailored dataset split strategy—distinguishing this approach from the most directly comparable FSL method for EEG MI, and claims superior accuracy across all three datasets.

3. **Focal loss for handling class imbalance in few-shot settings**: The relation module is trained with focal loss (Section 3.3, with explicit formula including α and γ parameters), which addresses the potential class imbalance issues in episodic few-shot training more effectively than standard cross-entropy.

4. **New 64-channel experimental dataset with controlled protocol**: Section 4.1.1 describes a newly collected dataset (7 participants, 64 active electrodes, EMG monitoring for movement artifact detection, publicly shared via Figshare), extending the available resources for high-density EEG MI research.

### Presentation / practical strengths

1. **Practical benefit for BCI calibration time**: The paper explicitly frames the contribution in terms of reducing recalibration burden for users (Section 1 and Section 4.4), noting this is particularly important for patients with disabilities who experience fatigue during extended training sessions.

## Summary

The paper proposes FSL-MIC, a few-shot learning framework for EEG motor imagery classification combining temporal convolution embeddings, a self-attention module, and a learned relation module to classify data from unseen subjects using only a few labeled support samples. The single most important piece of evidence is Table 1, which shows that the RelationNet-attention model with as few as 1 shot (63.1% on BCI 2a) matches the CNN-attention-Few baseline trained with 40 samples (62.8%), and with 20 shots substantially surpasses it (72.6% vs. 62.8%), while these gains replicate consistently across three datasets with different channel counts and experimental conditions.

